<section>
    <h3>Same request, different responses</h3>
    <pre><code class="language-php" data-trim data-line-numbers="1-5|2-3|5">
// Recorded: an asynchronous export polled until it is ready
$client->request('GET', 'https://api.example.com/exports/42')->toArray(); // 202
$client->request('GET', 'https://api.example.com/exports/42')->toArray(); // 200

// On replay: 202, then 200, then 200 (the last response is served again)
    </code></pre>
    <ul style="font-size: 0.65em;">
        <li class="fragment">Entries are <b>appended</b> in order, never overwritten, and replayed <b>in order</b></li>
        <li class="fragment">The replay cursor belongs to <b>the test</b>, not to the client: it survives the kernel reboot done by <code>KernelBrowser</code></li>
        <li class="fragment"><code>RetryableHttpClient</code> cancels the failed attempt: a 500 then a 200 only records the 200</li>
    </ul>
    <aside class="notes">
        <p>Last matching topic. The hardest one.</p>
        <p>[click] An export. The API says 202: not ready. Same request again: 200, ready. Same request, two responses.</p>
        <p>[click] On replay: 202, then 200. If the code asks again: 200 again.</p>
        <p>[click] So entries are added in order, never replaced, and replayed in order.</p>
        <p>[click] The position in the file belongs to the test, not to the client. Why? KernelBrowser restarts the kernel between two requests, and creates a new client. If not, we would replay 202 forever.</p>
        <p>[click] And with RetryableHttpClient: a 500, then a 200? Only the 200 is recorded.</p>
        <p>Each point here was a real bug, found during the review.</p>
    </aside>
</section>
